* tb_temp_core_loop_stability_postlayout -- HAND-MAINTAINED, not
* sim/build_tb.py output.
*
* POST-LAYOUT SIBLING (issue #298) of
* sim/temp-core-loop-stability/testbench/tb_temp_core_loop_stability.spice
* (issue #274, the schematic-level loop-break testbench) -- read that file's
* header first for the full method: why the loop is broken at PG, why PG
* itself stays put and only the two feedback-closing gates move, and the
* algebra showing the measured ratio T(jw) = V(xdut.pg)/V(pg_fb) does not
* depend on the injection source's polarity or amplitude. This file applies
* the identical break technique to the EXTRACTED netlist instead of the
* schematic export, so it cannot be sim/build_tb.py output either:
* build_tb.py only ever appends a cell VERBATIM after the stimulus, and this
* fragment edits lines *inside* the subcircuit body -- a verbatim copy has
* nothing to attach that edit to.
*
* ---- Forked from ------------------------------------------------------
*
* Forked from layout/postlayout/temp_core.spice (sha256
* 3478dd380f65b23d0a257c7b6948b24cbc395b475061bba1a584490570164e92)
*
* That digest is checked on every CI run by sim/tests/test_postlayout_forks.py:
* if layout/postlayout/temp_core.spice is regenerated (new layout, new klt)
* the test fails, naming this file, so the drift cannot go unnoticed the way
* it did between #314 and #316. `python3 sim/build_tb.py --check` cannot
* catch it -- this fragment is deliberately outside that mechanism, exactly
* like its schematic sibling. When it does fail: re-diff this file against
* the regenerated netlist, re-apply the four edits below by hand, and update
* the digest.
*
* ---- What changed vs layout/postlayout/temp_core.spice -------------------
*
* THE PARASITIC MODEL MATTERS HERE, so state it first. layout/postlayout.py
* models each net's drawn interconnect as one resistive STAR
* (layout/postlayout/AUDIT.md -> "The parasitic model"): the net's own name
* is the hub, each device terminal on it is a <net>__t<k> node, the net's one
* lumped series resistance is split across the R<net>_t<k> arms joining them,
* and its lumped capacitance sits on the hub. So PG's twelve device terminals
* are PG__t0 .. PG__t11, each behind its own arm to the hub node PG, and no
* device card names PG directly.
*
* In the extracted netlist, XMP1/XMP2 -- the schematic's names for the two
* mirror legs whose gates actually close the loop -- are each split by the
* layout into two parallel unit devices: XMP1 -> X36 + X47 (drain M1D),
* XMP2 -> X38 + X45 (drain M2D). XMP3, the dead-end PTAT-readout leg that
* does NOT feed back into the amplifier's own inputs (see the schematic
* fragment's header), is likewise split into X40 + X43 (drain M3D) and is
* deliberately left on PG here, exactly as the schematic fragment leaves
* XMP3 on PG. So this fork, applied to a verbatim copy of
* layout/postlayout/temp_core.spice, changes exactly:
*   1. `.subckt temp_core VDD VSS IBIAS EN PTAT CTAT` ->
*      `.subckt temp_core_loopbreak_postlayout VDD VSS IBIAS EN PTAT CTAT
*      PG_FB` (rename + one new port, so this can never be confused with the
*      real extracted temp_core.spice or accidentally instantiated in its
*      place)
*   2. `RPG_t7  PG__t7  PG 151.271388757` -> `... PG_FB`   (X47's gate arm)
*   3. `RPG_t8  PG__t8  PG 234.368666133` -> `... PG_FB`   (X45's gate arm)
*   4. `RPG_t10 PG__t10 PG 599.142193276` -> `... PG_FB`   (X36's gate arm)
*   5. `RPG_t11 PG__t11 PG 514.848951694` -> `... PG_FB`   (X38's gate arm)
*   6. `.ends temp_core` -> `.ends temp_core_loopbreak_postlayout`
*
* WHY THE ARMS AND NOT THE DEVICE CARDS. The four broken gates sit on
* PG__t7/t8/t10/t11, and each of those nodes is on exactly two cards: its own
* device and its own arm. Moving the DEVICE card's gate to PG_FB would leave
* its arm hanging off PG on a single card -- a node connected to one element,
* which is not what the break means and is worse conditioned besides. Moving
* the ARM's hub end instead keeps each gate behind its own drawn interconnect
* resistance, on the receiving side of the break where that metal physically
* is, and leaves no node with fewer connections than it had. Both spellings
* remove the same feedback path; only this one leaves the netlist a connected
* graph. (Issue #316, which re-derived the same hub-collapse reasoning for
* sim/por-brownout-slew/control/run_net_attribution.py's net-shorting
* manipulation.)
*
* X40 and X43 (M3D, the dead-end leg) are untouched: their arms RPG_t2 and
* RPG_t9 stay on PG, as do the other six PG arms (X7, X15, X49, X54, X55 and
* the XCC plate X65). Nothing else in the subcircuit body was touched -- in
* particular `CPG PG VSS 6.8139567e-14`, PG's lumped interconnect
* capacitance, and `Ccc_NZ_PG NZ PG`, its coupling to the compensation node,
* both stay on PG: that is the amplifier's own output metal, which is on the
* driving side of the break. PG_FB carries no interconnect model of its own
* (it is not a drawn net at all -- it exists only in this fork); its only
* load is the four gate arms above plus the injection source.
*
* Verified against the mapping the Curator spot-checked in issue #298's own
* review comment, re-applied to the regenerated netlist in #316.
*
* Netlist FRAGMENT: no .include / .lib / .temp / .control / .end -- the
* harness owns those (sim/harness/README.md). Parameters it supplies:
* vdd_val, vdd_nom, temp_c.

* ---- stimulus: one enabled DUT, PG broken out for AC injection ----------
* Identical to the schematic sibling's stimulus (same DUT port list and
* same 1 Tohm effectively-unloaded pad convention), except the instantiated
* subcircuit is this file's extracted temp_core_loopbreak_postlayout.
vsup  vdd 0 dc {vdd_val}
ven   en  0 dc {vdd_val}
xdut  vdd 0 ibias en ptat ctat pg_fb temp_core_loopbreak_postlayout
ibref vdd ibias dc 500n
Rlp   ptat 0 1T
Rlc   ctat 0 1T

* Loop-break injection: DC 0 (so the DC operating point is unaffected -- see
* the schematic sibling's header for the algebra, unchanged here) / AC 1
* (the small-signal stimulus the .ac sweep uses).
vac   pg_fb xdut.pg dc 0 ac 1

* temp_core_loopbreak_postlayout -- hand-forked from
* layout/postlayout/temp_core.spice (see header above for exactly what
* changed and why).
.subckt temp_core_loopbreak_postlayout VDD VSS IBIAS EN PTAT CTAT PG_FB

